# zensim NEON vs forced-scalar — first tier-isolated measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cd zensim-bench && cargo bench --bench tier_isolation
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
profile: ZensimProfile::B (current deterministic linear profile)

## Why this file exists

bench_zensim.rs measures absolute throughput; bench_compare.rs measures zensim
against other metrics. Neither can tell you whether the SIMD paths earn their
keep — a kernel slower than its own scalar fallback is invisible in both. This
bench runs the identical pipeline with the native SIMD token disabled.

## Result

| size       | NEON      | scalar    | speedup |
|------------|-----------|-----------|---------|
| 256x256    |  1.0676ms |  1.2521ms |  1.17x  |
| 512x512    |  2.4541ms |  3.0959ms |  1.26x  |
| 1920x1080  | 14.508ms  | 18.805ms  |  1.30x  |

No regression, but this is the WEAKEST SIMD payoff measured across the zen
crates on this host. For comparison, same machine, same method:

  butteraugli  3.4-3.7x     zenresize (linear) 4.0-4.3x
  linear-srgb  3.3-6.6x     fast-ssim2         1.7-1.8x
  zenwebp      1.5-1.6x     zensim             1.2-1.3x   <- here

butteraugli and zensim are both multi-scale perceptual metrics over XYB with
blur pyramids, so ~3x less SIMD benefit is a signal, not a property of the
problem. Two concrete things to check:

1. Coverage. zensim's kernels are declared `#[magetypes(neon, wasm128, scalar)]`
   — note there is no `v3`/`v4` in those lists at all, unlike every other zen
   crate. Whatever the reason on x86, it is worth confirming the NEON variants
   are actually reached for the hot kernels rather than only the few that
   carry the attribute (grep shows magetypes on 4 sites in fused.rs/color.rs
   while blur.rs, feature_v2.rs and simd_ops.rs carry the bulk of the work).
2. Attribution. These are whole-pipeline numbers. A profile is needed to say
   which stage is scalar-bound; the 372/924-feature extraction path is a
   plausible suspect since it is not obviously vectorised.

This is a LEAD, not a conclusion — nothing here is measured per kernel.
